What is containerd?

containerd is a container runtime — meaning it's responsible for managing the entire lifecycle of containers, including:
✅ Pulling images
✅ Creating containers
✅ Running/stopping containers
✅ Managing storage (snapshots)
✅ Handling networking
It was originally part of Docker but has since become a standalone project (now under the Cloud Native Computing Foundation — CNCF). Docker now uses containerd internally as its runtime.

🐳 How Docker Works (Simplified)
You type a Docker command, like:
-docker run nginx
•Docker CLI sends this request to the Docker daemon (dockerd).
-Docker Daemon:
•Uses Docker REST API to interpret the request.
•Delegates the actual container tasks to containerd.
-containerd:
•Pulls the image from a registry (if needed).
•Prepares the filesystem using overlayFS.
•Asks runc to create and start the container.
-runc:
•Uses Linux kernel features (namespaces, cgroups) to run the container process in isolation.
•The container starts! 🎉
•It appears as a separate process on the host, but it’s isolated from everything else.
